<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/advanced/behavioural-economics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Behavioural economics tests homo economicus against real humans — and finds humans
    <strong>predictably irrational</strong>: systematically biased, not randomly wrong. Kahneman
    and Tversky's prospect theory (1979) launched the field; their experiments documented the
    heuristics (mental shortcuts) that work well in nature and misfire in markets. The discipline
    now builds "choice architecture" that steers real people rather than idealised ones.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four heuristics every economist now checks for" widget="equationStep" config={behavCfg} />

  <h2>The findings that stuck</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Framing effects:</strong> "90% survival" and "10% mortality" are the same statistic — and produce different surgery choices. Doctors are not immune.</li>
    <li><strong>Mental accounting:</strong> money in labelled buckets behaves differently (tax refund splurges) — violating fungibility, confusing classical theory, describing real households.</li>
    <li><strong>Markets discipline some biases:</strong> repeated feedback and competition reduce anchoring in trading; experience helps. But rare-event misjudgment and myopia survive even among professionals.</li>
    <li><strong>The ethics debate is live:</strong> nudging is transparent and choice-preserving by definition — but "who designs the choice?" and for whose benefit are fair questions critics keep asking (and the answer changes by country).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kahneman & Tversky, Econometrica (1979); Thaler & Sunstein, Nudge (2008); Kahneman, Thinking, Fast and Slow (2011).</p>
</TopicPage>
